From: Shamik Saha <shamik.saha.rcciit@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 18:13:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] =?UTF-8?q?v0.3.3=20=E2=80=94=20F7:=20every=20euro=20c?=
 =?UTF-8?q?ap=20was=20inert=20unless=20the=20caller=20set=20cost=5Feur=20b?=
 =?UTF-8?q?y=20hand?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Euro caps are evaluated against `request.cost_eur`. Nothing derived that
from the action's parameters, and the LangGraph tool wrapper shipped in
`examples/` -- the one `docs/integration-langgraph.md` instructs people
to use -- never set it. So every request declared a cost of zero, every
euro cap compared `0 + 0 > limit`, and every payment passed.

Found by building an accounts-payable demo for a blog post. A EUR 500
daily budget, 22 payments, all under a EUR 100 per-payment threshold:

  no gate                EUR 4,367.59
  naive per-call limit   EUR 1,967.59
  onedoor (before)       EUR 1,967.59   <- identical to the naive gate
  onedoor (after)        EUR   462.65

This is worse than the five defects the bypass suite found. Those needed
adversarial input. This one is the documented happy path, it is silent,
and the audit trail it produces looks correct. Anyone who wrapped their
tools as the docs instruct and set a euro budget did not have one.

The fix, in the shape the rest of the engine already uses:

- `Policy.cost_param` names the parameter carrying the money, so the
  amount comes from policy -- the trusted input -- rather than from every
  integrator remembering an optional argument.
- An amount that cannot be resolved is a denial (`cost_unknown`), never
  an assumed zero. That includes an unset `cost_eur`, because a caller
  who never set it is indistinguishable from one who set it to zero.
- `cost_param` must also appear under `bounds.required`: a parameter that
  may be absent is not a source of truth for money.

Breaking, deliberately. A policy with a euro cap whose requests carry no
resolvable amount now denies where it previously permitted. That is the
same trade as F2 -- an unsafe default that depends on the author
remembering is the defect, not the ergonomics.

One existing test changed: `test_tier2_capped_executes` used a fixture
with euro caps and a request with no amount, and executed because of the
bug. It now supplies a real amount. That is the fourth test I have
changed to make a fix pass in this cycle, and each one deserves the same
hostile reading.

Seven regression tests. 122 pass. mypy unchanged at 7 pre-existing
errors. ruff gains one SIM118 on a `row.keys()` membership test, matching
the three identical lines beside it -- `sqlite3.Row` has no key-wise
`__contains__`, so the suggested rewrite would be wrong.
